    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1158">

                <pb facs="0955" n="839" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg839"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.113/930.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Consul at Nogales to the Secretary of
                        State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas Ariz.</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-09-08T16:00:00-07:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 8, 1912—4 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I have just forwarded to Naco the arms and ammunition for consular agent Cananea
                    by the same train it arrived on; it can not be forwarded immediately to Cananea
                    from Naco because about 200 rebels have taken possession of railroad. There are
                    now 500 Federals at Cananea, with 400 more en route to Cananea from Nogales;
                    apparently there is no danger of Cananea being captured, and arms and ammunition
                    can remain in the possession of United States troops Naco for the present.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dye.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
